WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.066 --> 00:00:01.067
Ron Binion: And ...

00:00:02.369 --> 00:00:05.071
Narrator: In this shot from
"Ghostbusters: Afterlife,"

00:00:05.071 --> 00:00:08.541
a Terror Dog stomps on Paul Rudd's car.

00:00:08.541 --> 00:00:10.276
But that's not CGI.

00:00:10.276 --> 00:00:11.945
It's actually a puppeteer

00:00:11.945 --> 00:00:14.614
wearing a mechanical Terror Dog arm

00:00:14.614 --> 00:00:17.417
controlled by a series of cables.

00:00:17.417 --> 00:00:19.452
To make creatures like the Terror Dog

00:00:19.452 --> 00:00:21.021
and this ghoulish skeleton

00:00:21.021 --> 00:00:23.356
hyperrealistic, makeup effects teams

00:00:23.356 --> 00:00:25.191
have to juggle visual design,

00:00:25.191 --> 00:00:27.761
classic puppeteering, and animatronics.

00:00:27.761 --> 00:00:29.729
Bill Sturgeon: Instead of being an actor

00:00:29.729 --> 00:00:31.031
and giving a performance,

00:00:31.031 --> 00:00:34.501
it's getting nine people
to get a performance.

00:00:36.369 --> 00:00:37.804
Narrator: Making practical creatures

00:00:37.804 --> 00:00:39.172
look hyperrealistic

00:00:39.172 --> 00:00:42.742
requires plenty of trial and
error in the design phase.

00:00:42.742 --> 00:00:45.779
This was especially true for "Afterlife."

00:00:45.779 --> 00:00:48.314
To stick to the spirit of
the original "Ghostbusters,"

00:00:48.314 --> 00:00:52.385
the team had to modernize the
original Terror Dog from 1984

00:00:52.385 --> 00:00:54.654
with as little CGI as possible.

00:00:54.654 --> 00:00:56.423
They started with these maquettes,

00:00:56.423 --> 00:00:59.259
miniature models used to
nail down a creature's look

00:00:59.259 --> 00:01:02.095
before creating the
actual on-screen model.

00:01:02.095 --> 00:01:04.230
Maquettes also give
the visual effects team

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:07.300
a real-life reference so
they can complete CG shots

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:09.502
that are too difficult to do practically,

00:01:09.502 --> 00:01:12.472
like when the Terror Dog
runs through Walmart.

00:01:12.472 --> 00:01:15.308
This is when Arjen runs
paint tests on a mock head

00:01:15.308 --> 00:01:18.011
to determine which colors
look best on camera.

00:01:18.011 --> 00:01:20.313
For example, his team
amped up the lip color

00:01:20.313 --> 00:01:21.414
in the final version.

00:01:22.382 --> 00:01:24.551
And a more detailed
reference head gives a sense

00:01:24.551 --> 00:01:26.653
of what it'll look like
under movie lighting.

00:01:26.653 --> 00:01:27.821
Arjen: So if he's in warm light,

00:01:27.821 --> 00:01:29.756
you can tell he is more brown,

00:01:29.756 --> 00:01:32.192
but if he's in cooler
light, he's more gray.

00:01:32.192 --> 00:01:33.093
Narrator: At the same time,

00:01:33.093 --> 00:01:35.728
"Afterlife" animatronic
designer Bill Sturgeon

00:01:35.728 --> 00:01:37.530
conducted a movement test

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:40.100
by connecting a scaled-down 2D printout

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:41.968
of the Terror Dog to brass tubing,

00:01:41.968 --> 00:01:44.304
a Popsicle stick, and plastic cables.

00:01:44.304 --> 00:01:47.040
This is how Sturgeon determines
the creature's biggest

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:50.810
and most notable movement,
known as the gross body move.

00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:53.346
Once a creature's look and
movements are solidified,

00:01:53.346 --> 00:01:54.881
the makeup effects team will then

00:01:54.881 --> 00:01:57.283
sculpt the final design true to size.

00:01:57.283 --> 00:01:59.586
For "Afterlife," they made
a larger model of the dog

00:01:59.586 --> 00:02:02.222
out of 600 pounds of clay,

00:02:02.222 --> 00:02:05.091
which served as the mold
for the actual animatronic.

00:02:05.091 --> 00:02:08.061
So it was key to get everything
right, down to the crevices

00:02:08.061 --> 00:02:09.762
on its horns and skin.

00:02:09.762 --> 00:02:12.031
Arjen cast the mold with foam latex,

00:02:12.031 --> 00:02:14.634
a departure from the modern
standard of silicone.

00:02:14.634 --> 00:02:16.803
We opted to go with foam
latex because of the weight,

00:02:16.803 --> 00:02:18.771
but it also moves great in compression.

00:02:18.771 --> 00:02:20.440
Silicone would have worked,

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:22.375
but it also would have made the Terror Dog

00:02:22.375 --> 00:02:24.978
about 500 pounds heavier
than it already is.

00:02:24.978 --> 00:02:27.680
Narrator: With the mechanics
and protective fiberglass shell,

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:31.084
the Terror Dog ultimately
weighed 450 pounds --

00:02:31.084 --> 00:02:34.487
still heavy, but light enough to maneuver.

00:02:34.487 --> 00:02:35.922
Softer foam latex

00:02:35.922 --> 00:02:38.091
wouldn't suffice for every
part of the creature,

00:02:38.091 --> 00:02:40.760
like its teeth, which
were made with acrylic.

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:41.895
Arjen: So they're actually hard. Yeah.

00:02:41.895 --> 00:02:45.098
You not want to get
bitten by one of these.

00:02:45.098 --> 00:02:47.700
Narrator: The acrylic created
a degree of translucency,

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:49.502
similar to a real set of teeth,

00:02:49.502 --> 00:02:52.505
but it would be too brittle
for the dog's nails,

00:02:52.505 --> 00:02:53.973
which needed to be strong enough

00:02:53.973 --> 00:02:56.509
to endure the physical
demands of shooting.

00:02:56.509 --> 00:02:58.044
So they used a urethane resin

00:02:58.044 --> 00:03:00.547
for light, tough, and hollow nails.

00:03:00.547 --> 00:03:01.381
Arjen: If they would get hit,

00:03:01.381 --> 00:03:02.815
or somebody would accidentally step on it,

00:03:02.815 --> 00:03:03.650
they wouldn't break.

00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:05.318
So there's a lot of
thought that went into it.

00:03:05.318 --> 00:03:06.653
Narrator: These horns were also made

00:03:06.653 --> 00:03:08.254
of the same resin as the nails,

00:03:08.254 --> 00:03:10.957
which let light in to look
more realistic on camera.

00:03:10.957 --> 00:03:11.791
Look at the difference

00:03:11.791 --> 00:03:13.793
between the horns made during paint tests

00:03:13.793 --> 00:03:15.161
and the final version.

00:03:15.161 --> 00:03:17.997
It just absorbs the light
like a real horn would.

00:03:17.997 --> 00:03:19.599
Narrator: The horns also
needed to be sturdy,

00:03:19.599 --> 00:03:21.267
since they needed to be removable.

00:03:21.267 --> 00:03:23.770
The crew only made one
Terror Dog animatronic

00:03:23.770 --> 00:03:26.839
to stand in for both
Zuul and Vinz Clortho,

00:03:26.839 --> 00:03:29.442
so they built the horns
with magnetic attachments,

00:03:29.442 --> 00:03:31.344
which could easily be swapped out.

00:03:31.344 --> 00:03:33.079
These longer horns were for the male,

00:03:33.079 --> 00:03:34.747
and these shorter ones were
for the female Terror Dog.

00:03:34.747 --> 00:03:38.318
But for practical creatures
to look convincing,

00:03:38.318 --> 00:03:41.054
they ultimately have
to move realistically.

00:03:42.589 --> 00:03:44.490
Unlike more traditional puppeteering,

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:47.193
animatronic work requires
mechanized motors

00:03:47.193 --> 00:03:48.494
known as servos,

00:03:48.494 --> 00:03:51.197
which can be programmed to
create specific movements.

00:03:51.197 --> 00:03:52.966
Instead of cables and strings,

00:03:52.966 --> 00:03:55.001
they're brought to life using controllers.

00:03:55.001 --> 00:03:55.835
For "Afterlife,"

00:03:55.835 --> 00:03:57.503
the Terror Dog would have to be designed

00:03:57.503 --> 00:03:59.572
to fit all the servos inside.

00:03:59.572 --> 00:04:01.107
And there were a lot of them.

00:04:01.107 --> 00:04:04.377
The Terror Dog had 21
servos in its face alone

00:04:04.377 --> 00:04:06.212
to allow for subtle expressions.

00:04:06.212 --> 00:04:07.280
And one mouth movement

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:09.349
could take three people to perform it.

00:04:09.349 --> 00:04:11.184
Bill: Because the mouth is so big,

00:04:11.184 --> 00:04:13.886
the servos have to move a lot of material

00:04:13.886 --> 00:04:15.021
to make it look right.

00:04:15.021 --> 00:04:16.990
Narrator: For example, the
servos needed the ability

00:04:16.990 --> 00:04:19.158
to rotate and move a lot of skin around

00:04:19.158 --> 00:04:20.893
for subtle movements such as blinking,

00:04:20.893 --> 00:04:22.495
squinting, and sniffing.

00:04:22.495 --> 00:04:25.265
So the foam latex was much
thinner in the face and neck

00:04:25.265 --> 00:04:26.165
than the body.

00:04:26.165 --> 00:04:28.334
Bill: If you know a part's going to move

00:04:28.334 --> 00:04:30.837
more than something else, you
might make deeper wrinkles.

00:04:30.837 --> 00:04:32.472
Narrator: You can see
that feature in the neck,

00:04:32.472 --> 00:04:35.775
which Arjen knew would be heavily
utilized, and gave Sturgeon

00:04:35.775 --> 00:04:38.177
the ability to imitate a
very specific head turn

00:04:38.177 --> 00:04:41.180
the original puppet did in 1984.

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:42.882
It could even look like it was actually

00:04:42.882 --> 00:04:45.551
eating out of a bag of dog
food during this moment.

00:04:45.551 --> 00:04:48.121
The Terror Dog also needed
to open its mouth wide,

00:04:48.121 --> 00:04:51.090
meaning placement of the jaw's
pivot point was also key.

00:04:51.090 --> 00:04:52.525
Arjen: We had to figure
that it can't be too high,

00:04:52.525 --> 00:04:54.427
can't be too low. It would look odd.

00:04:56.863 --> 00:04:59.065
Narrator: Going down the rest
of the animatronic's body,

00:04:59.065 --> 00:05:01.167
the foam latex could be much thicker,

00:05:01.167 --> 00:05:03.670
since it had less animatronic movement.

00:05:03.670 --> 00:05:06.406
But it still required
some crucial additions.

00:05:06.406 --> 00:05:09.442
Sturgeon placed a linear
actuator in the beast's stomach.

00:05:09.442 --> 00:05:12.945
This specific servo would only
push out in a straight line

00:05:12.945 --> 00:05:14.947
to mimic realistic breathing.

00:05:14.947 --> 00:05:17.450
This full-body shot of
the Terror Dog right here,

00:05:17.450 --> 00:05:20.219
that's actually CGI, as Arjen and his team

00:05:20.219 --> 00:05:22.588
never created its back half for real.

00:05:22.588 --> 00:05:25.024
Instead, the top half
was attached to a cart

00:05:25.024 --> 00:05:26.426
with two weights on it.

00:05:26.426 --> 00:05:29.195
The cart made it easier to
move the heavy puppet around

00:05:29.195 --> 00:05:32.165
so it could be placed on the
hood of a car in one shot.

00:05:32.165 --> 00:05:34.701
Puppeteers will also rely
on traditional cables

00:05:34.701 --> 00:05:36.169
when the movement calls for it.

00:05:36.169 --> 00:05:39.238
Like with this snarl, one of
the most complicated movements

00:05:39.238 --> 00:05:40.073
for the dog.

00:05:41.641 --> 00:05:43.710
Sturgeon used cables to work alongside

00:05:43.710 --> 00:05:46.679
the servos in the lips
to really nail the look.

00:05:46.679 --> 00:05:48.948
Bill: That's actually a number of servos

00:05:48.948 --> 00:05:50.483
working at the same time

00:05:50.483 --> 00:05:55.355
to give it a left and a
right side, and a snarl.

00:05:55.355 --> 00:05:57.590
Narrator: And in these quick
shots of a skeleton miner,

00:05:57.590 --> 00:05:59.759
the ghosts needed to handle a coffee mug.

00:05:59.759 --> 00:06:02.762
So creature designer Tanner
White designed this cable system

00:06:02.762 --> 00:06:05.765
that connected his hand
to the miner's hand.

00:06:05.765 --> 00:06:08.234
Tanner: Each of the
joints was the real limit

00:06:08.234 --> 00:06:09.836
of the actual hand joint.

00:06:09.836 --> 00:06:12.271
So it didn't move when it shouldn't.

00:06:12.271 --> 00:06:15.408
So each finger correlates
to the finger movement.

00:06:15.408 --> 00:06:18.511
And since you use your own hand strength,

00:06:18.511 --> 00:06:20.646
you can grasp things a lot stronger

00:06:20.646 --> 00:06:21.981
than you could with a radio control.

00:06:21.981 --> 00:06:23.883
To grip something like the cup here,

00:06:23.883 --> 00:06:25.752
basically you just pull all the fingers

00:06:25.752 --> 00:06:27.954
and the wrist movement
down so that he's grasping,

00:06:27.954 --> 00:06:30.523
and then if you let go? He drops it.

00:06:30.523 --> 00:06:31.924
Narrator: Giving life to these creatures

00:06:31.924 --> 00:06:33.960
can mean some very uncomfortable positions

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:34.861
for the puppeteers.

00:06:34.861 --> 00:06:37.730
While the miner's eyes were
radio-controlled off-camera,

00:06:37.730 --> 00:06:40.433
Arjen had the cable operate
its brows and other parts

00:06:40.433 --> 00:06:43.035
of its face through the
bottom of the puppet.

00:06:43.035 --> 00:06:45.037
That meant crouching
under the diner counter

00:06:45.037 --> 00:06:47.373
to avoid ending up on camera.

00:06:48.441 --> 00:06:50.510
For some moves, it's
easier just to puppeteer

00:06:50.510 --> 00:06:52.645
a single part of the anatomy on its own

00:06:52.645 --> 00:06:54.547
based on what's shown on camera.

00:06:54.547 --> 00:06:55.815
Like in the close-up shot

00:06:55.815 --> 00:06:57.784
where the Terror Dog stomps its foot down

00:06:57.784 --> 00:06:59.218
on the hood of Rudd's car.

00:06:59.218 --> 00:07:01.687
To get the full force,
Arjen created a separate

00:07:01.687 --> 00:07:04.957
Terror Dog leg made of
the exact same materials.

00:07:04.957 --> 00:07:07.427
Puppeteer Ron Binion grabbed
onto a metal armature

00:07:07.427 --> 00:07:09.362
inside the leg to move it.

00:07:09.362 --> 00:07:10.596
But the leg was so big

00:07:10.596 --> 00:07:12.732
that a human arm couldn't reach the foot.

00:07:12.732 --> 00:07:14.200
So cables were used to give

00:07:14.200 --> 00:07:16.269
convincing ankle and toe movements.

00:07:16.269 --> 00:07:17.670
Ron: When I'm moving it, I want to move it

00:07:17.670 --> 00:07:21.073
in arcs that are similar to
what the Terror Dog would do.

00:07:21.073 --> 00:07:23.042
On the day of shooting,
I was up pretty high,

00:07:23.042 --> 00:07:26.779
same way, and trying to
get myself out of the shot.

00:07:26.779 --> 00:07:30.082
I'm looking at a spot on the
car hood, where I'm aiming,

00:07:30.082 --> 00:07:31.918
but also trying to keep
myself out of the frame.

00:07:31.918 --> 00:07:32.752
And ...

00:07:35.021 --> 00:07:36.122
If I'm pushing down,

00:07:36.122 --> 00:07:39.992
you can still see the foot
adjusting and moving and flexing

00:07:39.992 --> 00:07:42.695
as the creature would be,
shifting on its weight

00:07:42.695 --> 00:07:44.597
and sort of coming down hard.

00:07:44.597 --> 00:07:46.732
Narrator: And just like an
actor, a practical creature

00:07:46.732 --> 00:07:49.969
needs some makeup before
being camera-ready.

00:07:49.969 --> 00:07:52.638
That can be as simple as a
glossy shine on the skin,

00:07:52.638 --> 00:07:54.907
or some drool.

00:07:54.907 --> 00:07:57.477
So the crew applied a
product called UltraSlime

00:07:57.477 --> 00:07:59.145
to the dog's lips on set.

00:07:59.145 --> 00:08:01.681
Arjen: So we would do
this just before the take

00:08:01.681 --> 00:08:02.515
and set it.

00:08:02.515 --> 00:08:04.050
So in other words, if
he would start moving,

00:08:04.050 --> 00:08:05.618
it would string and start stretching.

00:08:05.618 --> 00:08:07.353
And you can see it just starts dripping,

00:08:07.353 --> 00:08:08.855
but you've got to catch
it at the right moment,

00:08:08.855 --> 00:08:10.523
but that's the fun part about it.

00:08:13.059 --> 00:08:13.960
The actors loved it.

00:08:13.960 --> 00:08:15.695
I remember Paul Rudd jumping up and down.

00:08:15.695 --> 00:08:16.829
And Carrie being so,

00:08:16.829 --> 00:08:19.699
so happy to see something
physically on set,

00:08:19.699 --> 00:08:21.334
which is so rare these days.

